Rem ==================================================================
Rem terminal.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: the console itself
Rem
Rem THIS MACHINE DECLARES NO WORDS. It is where the runtime's raw
Rem console builtins are documented: Print, Input, InputLine and InKey,
Rem plus Args, which is core rather than terminal-owned but is read here
Rem more often than anywhere else. All five are callable from any
Rem program with no Include at all — including this file gives you
Rem nothing, and is not needed to use them. It exists so that a reader
Rem who goes looking for "how does a Shoddy program read a keystroke"
Rem finds an answer in a machine named for the question, rather than
Rem needing to already know that section 10 of docs/spec.html exists.
Rem
Rem WHY THIS IS NOT vt100.shoddy, which is the natural guess. The split
Rem is raw versus protocol. Reading a pending keystroke has nothing to
Rem do with VT100 — InKey is the same word on a terminal that has never
Rem heard of DEC — while INTERPRETING the arrow-key and PF-key escape
Rem sequences that come back is exactly what VT100 means, and that is
Rem EvalKey, which stays in vt100.shoddy. Likewise vt100.shoddy builds
Rem escape-code STRINGS and never prints one; Print is what puts a
Rem string on the screen and belongs here. The two cooperate — the
Rem usual shape is Print(CursorPos(1, 1) & ClearEos()) and
Rem EvalKey(InKey()) — but neither includes the other, and neither
Rem needs to.
Rem
Rem Everything here is EFFECTFUL, unlike almost every other machine in
Rem the library. By convention that keeps effects at the edges, these
Rem belong on Main's side of a program.
Rem ==================================================================

Rem ---- the builtins this machine documents ---------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused. The flat reference is section 10 of
Rem docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.

Rem Print(s As String)                                ( s -- )
Rem     Write s to the screen, followed by a newline. There is no
Rem     write-without-newline builtin, so a line assembled from several
Rem     pieces is built up with & and printed once — which is also what
Rem     vt100.shoddy's escape strings want, since several stray line
Rem     feeds mid-sequence are harmless on most terminals but are not
Rem     guaranteed to be.

Rem Input(prompt As String) As String                 ( prompt -- s )
Rem     Print the prompt, read one whole line, and answer it as a
Rem     String. Numbers come back through Val — or better, IsNumeric and
Rem     then Val. It collapses end of stream and a blank line into the
Rem     same "", which is what InputLine exists to separate.

Rem InputLine(prompt As String) As Array Of Number    ( prompt -- pair )
Rem     The same read, answering a 2-element ARRAY [atEof, text]:
Rem     element 1 a Boolean, True only at genuine end of stream, and
Rem     element 2 the line, empty at EOF. A program reading a redirected
Rem     script can reprompt on a blank line or stop at EOF; with Input
Rem     it can do one or the other but never both. It is a flat Array
Rem     rather than a record because a builtin cannot reach a Type —
Rem     lifting it into a sum type is the caller's job, and
Rem     mills/halifax does exactly that in HxAskedOf.

Rem InKey() As String                                 ( -- s )
Rem     One pending keystroke, read without blocking and without echo,
Rem     or "" when nothing is pending. Arrow keys and PF1-PF4 arrive as
Rem     their VT100 application-mode escape sequences, which is what
Rem     vt100.shoddy's EvalKey is for: EvalKey(InKey()) is a working
Rem     keyboard poll for a game loop. See mills/pac-vt100.

Rem Args() As List Of String                          ( -- args )
Rem     The arguments the program was started with.
Rem
Rem     ALSO AVAILABLE IN NATIVE HOSTS — not terminal-exclusive. Unlike
Rem     the four above, a terminal is not this word's only home: a
Rem     headless service or a program launched from a desktop has
Rem     arguments too, with no console involved anywhere. It is
Rem     documented here because reading your own invocation is a
Rem     console-shaped thing to want, and spec.html classifies it as
Rem     core, not as this machine's. Nothing needs including to call it.
Rem ==================================================================
